12. SECOND SCIENTIFIC AUDIT: STUDENT POLICY, PERCEPTION, AND LAUNCH TOPOLOGY
======================================================================

The long-running r20 job remained numerically healthy, but a deeper audit found
two scientific contract violations that made its results unsuitable as the
final experiment:

- Adaptive KL control wrote the actor-selected learning rate into both the
  actor and critic optimizers. By iteration 5100 both effective rates were
  `1e-5`, even though the critic has an independent configured rate. The update
  path now changes only the actor optimizer and actor LR. During pure BC, KL is
  diagnostic-only; on resume, actor and critic rates are restored independently.
- `far_tracking_warp` advertised camera sensor noise and reset-pose
  randomization without consistently applying the sampled runtime state. All
  camera backends now apply noise exactly once, partial environment slices use
  matching sampled tensors, and far-tracking capture reads the latest reset
  position/quaternion offsets. Startup validates the effective std, dropout,
  translation, and rotation tensors and fails closed if configured state is
  absent.

The student-policy path was re-audited end to end:

- iteration-zero schedule refresh occurs before rollout; a fresh run therefore
  uses `ppo_coeff=0`, deterministic student mean actions, and pure actor BC;
- teacher labels and student inputs are derived from the same pre-step state;
- the critic still performs value backward, distributed reduction, and an
  optimizer step during the pure-BC actor phase;
- the first nonzero PPO tier switches to stochastic student actions and only
  then enables adaptive actor-KL control;
- checkpoints retain independent optimizers/LRs, `iter`/`next_iter`, all
  rank-local environment and fixed-BC states, and recompute the schedule from
  `next_iter` on resume;
- teacher and policy-initializer checkpoints were recursively scanned and all
  tensors were finite. Their observation/action/module contracts match the
  effective student profile exactly. A prior formal checkpoint also confirmed
  that the perception CNN receives and applies BC gradients.

Low-priority robustness follow-ups were recorded but do not block a fresh run:
resume could additionally assert `next_iter == iter + 1` and finite/in-range
optimizer LRs; policy-init loading could recursively scan finite values itself;
and an end-to-end `_update_algo_step` unit test could directly assert critic
parameter movement and perception gradients.

Launcher topology now fails on the controller before snapshot installation,
SSH, intent publication, or GPU use when any of these contradictions exist:

- Gloo gradient reduction and hierarchical reduction are both enabled;
- CPU leader reduction is enabled without hierarchical reduction;
- hierarchical reduction is requested for a single node or one rank per node.

The launcher contract suite now covers invalid single-node/one-rank hierarchy,
contradictory modes, valid 2x2 hierarchy, valid single-node flat Gloo, and the
requirement that these errors precede all remote mutation. The full launcher
suite passed twice after the change.

The video-evaluation-only compatibility path was also reviewed. The opt-in
`HOLOSOMA_EVAL_ALLOW_MISSING_CAMERA_SENSOR_NOISE_STATE` is injected only into
video child processes and is never forwarded by the training launcher. Normal
training stays fail-closed. Tests explicitly prove all three cases: default
missing noise state fails; video opt-in may continue without a randomizer; and
even with the opt-in, a configured randomization range whose sampled tensor is
missing still fails. Removing contact-export reward terms and rollout-reference
roots is likewise confined to an explicit video-eval flag and does not alter
policy actions or training configuration.

The control account's W&B netrc file was found with mode 0644. It was changed to
0600 and its parser/authentication path was verified without printing or
copying credentials.

Targeted final regression for the late perception/eval delta passed 45 tests.
The broader final results remain: 522 Python tests passed (3 skipped), the PPO
module and hierarchy suites passed, the full launcher suite passed twice,
shell/Python syntax and `git diff --check` passed, and the immutable Python/NCCL
runtime closures were verified.

