31. CONTACT-OBSERVATION COVERAGE AND ONNX METADATA UNIQUENESS
======================================================================

An explicitly configured contact sidecar root is part of the student input
semantics whenever an actor/critic observation consumes contact-aware sparse
root or pickup/drop window terms.  Previously, direct checkpoint/evaluation
entrypoints that did not inherit the launcher's ambient coverage flag could
load zero or only part of the active clip bank and silently replace missing
windows with a kinematic estimate.  That changes a saved policy input without
changing its apparent tensor shape.

Code now automatically requires 100% active-clip window coverage when both a
nonempty root and a contact-window observation consumer are configured.  The
legacy no-root kinematic fallback remains intentional, and partial banks remain
valid for sampler/metrics-only users that do not feed the windows to a policy.
Regression tests cover zero-of-one and one-of-two configured observation banks.
The focused command file passed 28 tests; the complete contact/timestep/reset/
reward/checkpoint/rank review passed 229 tests.  The half-open `[t1,t2)`, FPS
ceil conversion, runtime-prepend compensation, reset-before-resample outcome,
reward/termination order, rollout label alignment and rank-local resume mapping
were also rechecked end to end.

ONNX `metadata_props` was another silent ambiguity boundary.  The SDK loader
previously assigned properties into a dictionary, so a duplicate key used
consumer-dependent last-wins semantics.  Python's default JSON parser also
accepts non-standard `NaN` and positive/negative `Infinity`.  The loader now
rejects empty or duplicate keys, malformed JSON and every non-finite JSON
constant before constructing an ONNX Runtime session.

The training-side metadata publisher now first validates all pre-existing
properties as unique, nonempty, strict finite JSON.  Updating an existing key
replaces it instead of appending a second entry, and new values use
`json.dumps(..., allow_nan=False)`.  Any validation/serialization failure leaves
the original ONNX bytes unchanged, so a producer cannot report success for an
artifact the SDK will deterministically reject.

