33. FINAL STUDENT-POLICY NUMERICS, RNG BOUNDARY AND PROXY CONTRACTS
======================================================================

Rollout action selection and the actor objective previously disagreed for a
positive Python PPO coefficient that rounds to zero in the float32 graph.
PPO presence, teacher-control legality, stochastic student actions and the PPO
loss multiplier now use one operational float32 coefficient.  Positive start,
target or first discrete PPO tiers that materialize as zero are rejected.
The BC complement is still computed in Python before its own float32
materialization, preserving the real positive BC semantics near
`nextafter(1, 0)`.

The initial BC tier alone was not sufficient.  A smallest-positive float32
`dagger_loss_coef` could be operational at iteration zero yet underflow to zero
later in the ramp, silently ending DAgger before its declared boundary.  For a
target below one, setup now checks the persistent end tier; for a target of
one it checks the final mathematically positive `end-1` tier.  Linear and
stepped schedules were independently tested for off-by-one behavior and legal
pure-BC boundary cases.

Required ONNX export is now validated before train mode, canonical reset or
rollout for every objective, including pure BC.  Recurrent `time_gru` and a
Flow actor with positive inference noise fail immediately rather than after a
full training run whose final export would represent a different policy.

Flow configuration no longer truncates booleans/floats or accepts strings,
NaN, Infinity, negative values and unbounded work.  Pydantic config, direct
`ConditionalFlowMLP`, checkpoint/resume validation and launcher preflight now
share `flow_integration_steps <= 4096` and noise std `<= 1e18`; epsilon remains
in `[0,0.49]`.  The noise bound keeps approximately an 18-sigma squared sample
below float32 maximum.  Independent stress tests kept 240,960 sampled losses
finite, and a 4096-step forward completed with finite output.  Values 4097 or
`1e19` fail before snapshot/SSH.

Checkpoint publication used to validate live state before capturing RNG.  A
custom state-dict/validation hook could therefore consume randomness and move
both the serialized and next-training boundary.  Every rank now captures its
RNG state first, then runs validation, environment/fixed-BC collection,
serialization and publication inside one `finally` restoration scope.
Injected success and failed-preflight side effects prove Python, NumPy and
torch streams all resume at the exact pre-save boundary.

IsaacSim proxy accessors were also reconciled with the environment-major,
interleaved `ObjectRegistry` layout.  Bounds errors are explicit, clone order
matches the registry contract, and reset/state-accessor mocks test the real
ordering rather than a stale object-major assumption.

