======================================================================
34. DIRECT RUNSIM CAMERA RESET RANDOMIZATION AND EPISODE-PAIRING CLOSURE
======================================================================

Direct RunSim previously instantiated a perception producer without replaying
the training environment's reset camera randomizer.  It could therefore render
from one fixed mount and omit the per-episode multiplicative depth-noise scale
and pixel-drop probability even when the student artifact had been trained
with all three effects.  The direct config and `mj_track.sh` handoff now carry
the authenticated camera translation XYZ ranges, rotation RPY ranges,
`noise_std_mult_range`, `noise_drop_prob_range`, producer tick period and seed
from the selected ONNX metadata.  Explicit launcher values must match the
artifact; missing, legacy, non-finite, reversed, out-of-domain, duplicate-term
or lifecycle-incompatible contracts fail before run_sim is launched.

RunSim now builds the real `RandomizationManager` with the canonical
`randomize_camera_raycast` reset term.  Every physical episode first clears the
perception history, then samples the mount translation/orientation and the two
noise parameters, and finally renders the targeted reset frame.  The live
camera path applies translation in the mount frame and adds RPY jitter without
rotating the mount lever arm.  Sensor noise is the training expression
`depth + randn * (depth * std_mult)`; dropout independently replaces selected
pixels with the configured maximum sensor distance.  Both are applied once
before observation preprocessing.  The direct producer is clocked by the
authenticated training control-step period rather than render-loop speed and
fails if a producer tick is skipped.

The initialization lifecycle now matches `BaseTask.reset_all`: reset manager
state, sample reset randomization, execute one isolated nominal-PD control tick,
perform the ordinary perception update, then install the targeted reset
output.  External bridge input is detached during that warm-up and its control
phase is cleared before and after, so queued lowcmd cannot replace the training
zero-action tick or leave a nonzero decimation phase.  Reset output may be
republished while physics is frozen, but this reads the cached frame and never
advances perception state or consumes another RNG sample.  The contract is
honestly distribution-equivalent: vectorized training and one-env RunSim use
different global-RNG call interleavings, so a shared seed does not promise the
same batch sample path.

Reset transport races were closed end-to-end.  Each run_sim producer starts
from a random bounded 62-bit episode base and increments it at every physical
reset, preventing a restarted producer from reusing old `(generation, t=0)`
identity.  Sim-state, lowcmd and every ZMQ/SHM perception frame carry this same
generation.  The SHM generation is committed in the same odd/even seqlock as
payload, simulator time and checksum; reset atomically invalidates the current
slot before advancing generation, while the writer's private sequence remains
monotonic.  WBT pins one validated sim-state snapshot per control tick and the
policy accepts only a perception frame with both `time <= pinned time` and an
exact generation match.  Missing generation now fails closed rather than
falling back to an unpaired latest frame.

The external relay preserves generation on both transports, publishes no
unauthenticated filler, never refreshes the wall age of a disconnected ZMQ
source, keys SHM freshness by `(segment generation, sequence)`, and includes
episode/time in its duplicate-frame identity.  Thus identical pixels at a new
episode and sequence 2 from a recreated SHM segment are still forwarded.  A
file source must explicitly declare a one-episode target generation.  External
renderers must carry the *target run_sim* generation; an unrelated local
counter is rejected, and the launcher now states this requirement.

Verification after the final transport fixes included 366 manager/direct/
eval/student/reset/protocol tests, 284 direct/student/transport tests, 37
invalidate/concurrency producer tests and an independent adversarial set of 61
tests; all passed.  The real MuJoCo ONNX metadata launcher suite ended with
`[PASS] MuJoCo perception metadata launcher contracts`.  Python compilation,
`bash -n`, `git diff --check`, concurrent marker/generation seqlock pairing and
same-name SHM restart tests also passed.

The scientific guarantee here is the authenticated direct `ZMQ + MuJoCo
Classic` path.  An external relay still cannot independently prove that a
source's generation belongs to the target simulator without subscribing to
target sim-state; mismatch is safe but currently manifests as a policy
timeout.  A single-slot SHM reader that starts only after its required frame
has been overwritten by a future same-episode frame also times out rather than
substituting the future frame.  These are fail-closed liveness limits, not
cross-episode correctness failures.  Generic DDS and MuJoCo Warp physical-reset
equivalence were not claimed by this closure.

