======================================================================
48. HIERARCHICAL TRAINING DATA/CONTROL PLANE (WORKSPACE CANDIDATE)
======================================================================

At 2026-07-16 UTC the performance work moved from scalar synchronization
cleanup to the two measured infrastructure bottlenecks.  The active formal
session `as_debug30_ws104_r22f_20260716_020038` still executes only its sealed
immutable source snapshot and its original CPU-leader environment.  No GPU,
remote node, live process, profiler, source deployment or running-job setting
was touched by this workspace work.

Historical phase evidence shows why this is material.  The current protocol
performs 64 gradient reductions and originally about 448 small control
collectives per iteration.  The CPU-leader gradient block measured about
50.84 ms per minibatch, or 3.254 seconds per iteration.  The closest historical
flat-NCCL observation measured about 17.16 ms per minibatch, but it came from a
different source/fail-closed protocol and only one completed training
iteration, so it is an upper-bound anchor rather than an A/B result.  Current
control collectives cost roughly 10--15 ms each.  Combining those observations
supports a realistic pre-canary total-iteration saving of about 2.8--5.5
seconds (roughly 1.19x--1.46x throughput at the current 17.5--17.8 s cadence),
not a production claim.

The candidate keeps the existing node-local NCCL reduction but allows the 13
node leaders to reduce the gradient payload directly on GPU by setting
`HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_GRAD_REDUCE_CPU_LEADER=0`.  This removes the per-
minibatch GPU-to-CPU copy, 13-leader Gloo payload reduction and CPU-to-GPU copy.
The GPU-leader unit contract proves the healthy-path order local NCCL reduce ->
leader NCCL all-reduce -> local Gloo barrier -> local NCCL broadcast, with no
detach/cpu/to/copy path and no fallback to the flat world group.  It does not
prove immediate method-level propagation of a failed leader to nonleaders;
that failure-boundary gap is addressed by the explicit subgroup timeout work
recorded below.

The new explicit `HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_SMALL_COLLECTIVES` flag is default
false.  With Gloo small collectives and hierarchical gradient topology enabled,
int32/int64 verdicts use node-local Gloo reduce -> 13-leader Gloo all-reduce ->
node-local Gloo broadcast.  There is deliberately no extra local barrier.
Floating KL, BC denominators, metrics, bool, complex and narrower integer
tensors retain the established flat Gloo path, preserving the floating
reduction tree.  GPU-gradient mode creates both the leader NCCL payload group
and a separate leader Gloo control group in one deterministic order.

Before any subgroup creation, every distributed rank now executes one default-
group agreement check over the frozen flags, global world/rank and preserved
rank-visible local topology.  A divergent flag, world, local world or local rank
therefore makes all ranks fail before `new_group`, instead of leaving peers to
time out in different group orders.  Single-rank evaluation safely ignores an
inherited training-only hierarchy flag.  Once a collective starts there is no
backend fallback.

Launcher help/defaults, strict boolean parsing, dependency validation, remote
export and per-node startup output expose the new setting and its integral-only
scope.  The flag is part of the execution-runtime fingerprint and exact resume
identity; enabling it requires `HOLOSOMA_GLOO_SMALL_COLLECTIVES=1` and
`HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_GRAD_REDUCE=1`, but does not require a CPU gradient
leader.  An older provenance-v2 payload cannot have enabled this previously
nonexistent path, so absence of this one additive field is canonically migrated
to false.  Explicit true, partial dependencies, all other missing/extra fields
and runtime drift remain fail-closed.

The exact future canary profile is therefore:

  HOLOSOMA_GLOO_GRAD_REDUCE=0
  HOLOSOMA_GLOO_SMALL_COLLECTIVES=1
  HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_GRAD_REDUCE=1
  HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_SMALL_COLLECTIVES=1
  HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_GRAD_REDUCE_CPU_LEADER=0

`scripts/debug_nccl_allreduce.py` was reduced to a focused 376-line harness. It
preserves the legacy environment interface, reproduces both CPU/GPU leader
gradient protocols, interleaves flat or hierarchical integer controls, checks
finite/value agreement, and reports warmup-excluded all-rank p50/p95/p99/max.
A real four-process loopback Gloo test ran 96 mixed SUM/MIN/MAX reductions over
int32/int64 scalar/vector payloads on a logical 2x2 topology, and the harness
also passed a real two-process CPU/Gloo smoke.  The merged PPO/provenance/resume
regression passed 278 tests; the harness contract adds 11 passes.  Launcher
default, enabled GPU-leader, missing-dependency and invalid-boolean dry runs all
returned the expected result.  Shell syntax, Python compilation and diff checks
passed.

This candidate remains disabled and unlaunched.  Release still requires an
independent 13x8 stress run using the pinned NCCL runtime, zero timeouts/rank
lag/non-finite results, followed by same-source/same-seed CPU-leader versus
GPU-leader simulator canaries.  Only measured p50/p95/p99 and end-to-end
iteration improvement may decide activation; the historical estimates above
must not be reported as achieved speedup.

======================================================================
49. STUDENT-UPDATE HOT PATH AND DISTRIBUTED FAILURE ATOMICITY
======================================================================

At 2026-07-16 UTC the next workspace candidate removed host synchronization
from the diagnostic side of every PPO minibatch without weakening training
checks.  Loss values now remain as detached device scalars through the full
optimizer loop.  At the iteration boundary they are grouped by device, copied
to the host in bulk, and added as Python floats in the historical minibatch
order.  Non-scalar diagnostics still take their mean in the original dtype.
The batched finite check reports the exact invalid field with one boolean-vector
transfer per device rather than one `.item()` per field.  Complex diagnostics
are rejected explicitly instead of being cast to real and silently losing the
imaginary component.

Forward/loss construction, finite validation and backward now share one
all-rank outcome verdict.  The local loss region has no collective: KL is
computed locally; distributed KL payload construction, loss-weight lookup,
shape/device allocation and finite validation all finish before the verdict;
the actual KL SUM runs only after the synchronized gradient reduction and
before clipping/optimizer mutation.  Adaptive actor LR therefore still changes
before the actor optimizer step, while neither the current backward graph nor
gradient reduction observes a different LR.  A failed rank cannot leave peers
inside an earlier KL collective.  Healthy ranks that completed ordinary or
streamed partial backward clear actor and critic gradients when any peer fails.

DAgger controls use a rollout-selected authoritative mode rather than deciding
from each rank's minibatch mapping.  In the contiguous production path every
rank skips fallback count collectives; a missing local denominator/presence
raises inside the collective-free compute region and joins the common verdict.
Randomized/recurrent fallback ranks all enter the same count protocol, with
both a local preflight verdict and a post-count finalization verdict.  Thus a
rank-local error in teacher-mask preparation, host presence extraction,
mapping construction or `torch.where` cannot strand peers in a different
collective sequence.

Two real CPU/Gloo ranks validated the protocol: rank zero injected an actor
forward failure while rank one completed local backward.  Both ranks received
the same all-rank failure envelope, both parameter sets remained byte-equal to
their initial values, and all gradients were cleared.  Unit coverage also
injects a KL-payload allocation failure before any gradient collective and a
post-count DAgger fallback failure after the count SUM.

The same hot-path pass batches rollout logging reductions by device, gates
fixed-BC evaluation to its configured cadence plus the forced terminal proof,
and removes repeated std-cap `.item()` calls after the one-time activation
announcement.  Step-level heartbeat logging now depends only on
`HOLOSOMA_DEBUG_HEARTBEAT_VERBOSE`; the coarse heartbeat flag no longer emits
four messages and a reset-count device synchronization on every environment
step.  At the active formal topology, the old coarse/verbose mix could create
about 9,984 extra log messages and 2,496 reset-count host synchronizations per
iteration.  These changes affect only future snapshots; the sealed r22 source
and its live processes were not edited, restarted, profiled or attached.

The merged PPO/student/distill/fixed-BC/schedule/resume/rollout/logging/
heartbeat/motion/provenance selection passed 873 tests.  After the final
fallback/provenance compatibility edits, the affected PPO core and provenance
selection passed 541 tests.  The real two-rank failure test, focused motion
suite, and minibatch launcher contract passed independently.  Python compile,
shell syntax and full `git diff --check` also passed.

======================================================================
50. MOTION TELEMETRY SPLIT, BOUNDED SUBGROUPS AND EXPLICIT THROUGHPUT A/B
======================================================================

The configured motion-metrics interval previously had no effect whenever an
enabled WObject curriculum consumed one `motion/*` key: the command recomputed
all tracking, lift/contact and adaptive-sampler diagnostics on every environment
step.  The future candidate now refreshes only the enabled curriculum's exact
tracking-error key every step.  Full diagnostics follow
`HOLOSOMA_MOTION_METRICS_INTERVAL`.  The supported live set is the 13 reference,
body, joint and object tracking errors; any empty, misspelled, lift, sampler or
otherwise unsupported similarity key fails at environment setup.  Selective
values are new tensors, and direct lift views are cloned before the logging
meter retains them.

The BaseTask ordering remains termination -> reward -> pre-reset metric update
-> command reset -> curriculum reset.  Reward, BadTracking/motion-end
termination, reset sampling, AS exposure/failure state and clip weighting read
live command/simulator state rather than the throttled diagnostics.  The split
therefore leaves training, curriculum and RNG mathematics unchanged.  W&B
diagnostics intentionally become interval sample-and-hold telemetry.  Focused
motion, AS and checkpoint coverage passed 100 tests, and an independent
consumer/alias audit found no training-semantic dependency on the throttled
fields.

Every hierarchical NCCL/Gloo subgroup is now created with the frozen
`HOLOSOMA_HIERARCHICAL_PG_TIMEOUT_SEC` value, default 300 seconds, and that
value is part of execution provenance and exact-resume drift validation.
Launcher defaults enable the PyTorch NCCL watchdog/monitoring with a 300-second
heartbeat and 65,536-event trace buffer.  NCCL socket retry defaults are
34 attempts at 100 ms rather than the former 120 attempts at 1,000 ms; the
latter triangular backoff could wait about 121 minutes before surfacing an
unreachable peer.  `TORCH_DIST_TIMEOUT_SEC=3600` remains the separate default-
group/checkpoint/I/O bound.  Legacy provenance that predates the subgroup
timeout safely normalizes to 300.  Targeted provenance coverage passed 134
tests; default, explicit 53-second and invalid-zero launcher paths were checked.
The monolithic launcher suite was deliberately interrupted after its earlier
fixtures rather than consuming another roughly 30 minutes, so a future release
still needs the normal full launcher gate.

Finally, the launcher exposes a real throughput experiment without silently
changing the reference algorithm.  The default remains 64 minibatches.  The
16-minibatch candidate requires both
`HOLOSOMA_MINIBATCH_THROUGHPUT_CANARY=1` and explicit
`NUM_MINI_BATCHES=16`, plus one learning epoch.  The horizon is parsed from the
authenticated source snapshot's `PPOConfig.num_steps_per_env`; non-divisible
rollouts fail before launch or `all` performs any remote preparation.  Startup
logs show local/global rollout size, samples per minibatch update and update
rounds.  The canary label is execution provenance; old v2 checkpoints that
could not have enabled it normalize a missing field to false.

For the formal 104-rank, 64-environment, 24-step geometry, one iteration has
1,536 local and 159,744 global samples.  The reference performs 64 minibatch
update rounds with 24 local / 2,496 global samples each.  The opt-in canary
performs 16 rounds with 96 local / 9,984 global samples each.  It covers the
same rollout once but changes Adam step count, PPO update granularity and the
resulting trajectory; it is explicitly marked `math_equivalent=0`.  Based on
the observed fixed-latency dominance, it is the highest-leverage next A/B, but
no speedup is claimed until a same-source canary measures end-to-end iteration
time, learning curves, fixed-BC guard behavior and checkpoint health.

No new job or W&B run was launched.  The active immutable formal run remains
session `as_debug30_ws104_r22f_20260716_020038`, W&B
`zihanw22/carry-any/fdbn50pr`.

