======================================================================
53. SIMULATOR/COLLECTION PROFILE, CORRECTNESS FIXES AND PAIRED A/B
======================================================================

The optimization focus was moved from the already-small neural-network update
section to the simulator/collection critical path.  `batch_ne.sh` gained a
bounded, default-off collection timing gate:

  HOLOSOMA_COLLECTION_PROFILE_CANARY=0|1
  HOLOSOMA_COLLECTION_PROFILE_SYNC_CUDA=0|1
  HOLOSOMA_COLLECTION_PROFILE_INTERVAL=<positive integer>

CUDA synchronization is accepted only together with the explicit canary,
profiling is fresh-run only, the target is bounded to at most 64 iterations,
and node launch payloads first unset ambient values before exporting the
controller's canonical values.  The first diagnostic launch found that the
idle nodes lacked the exact NCCL payload and exited before any training worker
was created; the transactional launcher released all reservations/sessions.
After installing and verifying the required NCCL SHA256
`e4a7aee9c3eecf53fac780441d2f03b578ab8db8874b71f8e391bcec7adb2899`,
the exact old-source profile completed normally.

That profile was session
`as_debug30_ws24_b56_collprof_sync_r1_20260716_2333`, W&B
`zihanw22/carry-any/ze5mrmpu`, immutable source
`src-b56f370d2ac85e47ce2bf777ecc77aa0cd32ecfd684851cb6e7596f0fc73e1bc`,
three nodes x eight L40S GPUs, 64 env/GPU, seed 42, MB16 and 16 iterations.
The intrusive synchronized timing was used only for attribution.  Across
steady iterations 5--15, mean rollout was 4775.848 ms and training was
641.461 ms.  Mean environment-step time was 3915.579 ms.  Within collection,
physics was 1368.785 ms; write-data-to-sim was 591.720 ms; sim.step was
604.690 ms; perception was 100.218 ms; reward was 331.994 ms; offline contact
guidance was 105.835 ms; log update was 234.162 ms; motion metrics were
228.504 ms; tasks were 138.276 ms; normal observations were 462.285 ms;
final observations were 445.679 ms on the iterations where they occurred;
reset refresh was 470.355 ms; and total post-physics was 2533.675 ms.  Thus
collection, not model forward/backward, was the approximately 85--89% Amdahl
floor.

The audit found and fixed the following collection-path issues:

1. IsaacLab's generic ContactSensor performed a CUDA `nonzero()` host
   synchronization both in every physics-step `update()` and on every `.data`
   read.  Holosoma's fixed all-env/every-physics-step sensors now use an eager
   compatible fast path with fixed-shape device updates and automatically fall
   back for incompatible history/update-period configurations.  Partial reset
   now clears the main source sensor as well as filtered sensors before the
   derived history refresh, preventing previous-episode contact history from
   being copied straight back into reset rows.  A repository-wide consumer
   audit also proved that air/contact-time buffers were unused, so
   `track_air_time` is disabled and its norm/where kernels and four timer
   buffers are removed.

2. The G1 articulation instantiated 29 separate IdealPD actuator groups,
   forcing 29 Python dispatches and hundreds of tiny kernels per physics
   substep.  It now uses one all-joints group while exact escaped-name maps
   preserve each joint's effort limit, velocity limit, armature and friction;
   stiffness/damping remain zero.  Identity DOF mappings use a cached
   `slice(None)` view for state/torque hot paths, but only when the mapping
   covers the articulation's complete joint count; permutations and leading
   subsets preserve their explicit selector.  P/T control no longer copies or
   resets the action-term previous-velocity buffer that only V control reads.

3. Every object state property previously traversed `AllRootStatesProxy`,
   whose access path performed two device boolean reductions and a Python
   `.item()`.  MotionCommand now refreshes one active-object `[env,13]`
   snapshot per control phase, and all position/quaternion/velocity consumers,
   perception, reset, clip rollover and curriculum-assist writes share and
   update that snapshot.  Single-object IsaacSim uses its direct state adapter;
   multi-object/other backends retain a safe fallback.

4. A partial reset previously performed the full 64-environment dynamic-mesh
   depth raycast and sliced the result.  The fixed-shape Warp graph now owns a
   device int32 active-environment mask, and every depth kernel returns before
   ray or mesh access for inactive rows.  Full capture fills the mask; targeted
   reset capture updates only the requested rows.  The graph launches on the
   current PyTorch CUDA stream so pose/mask writes, Warp execution and Torch
   reads are ordered even on a non-default stream.  Normal training no longer
   clones large debug tensors unless a debug dump is actually configured.

5. Ordinary non-timeout terminals previously recomputed every observation as
   if it were needed for timeout bootstrap, and stale `final_observations`
   could survive in the reused extras dictionary.  Final observations are now
   transition-local, computed only when a timeout exists, and stored only for
   timeout rows that remain eligible after WBT's preview validation.

6. Adaptive-sampling interval mass/entropy metrics were vectorized and share
   one effective bin-probability construction, removing per-bin `.item()`
   loops without changing the configured intervals.

The combined local regression was `739 passed, 1 expected GPU-only skip` in
29.41 seconds.  Independent focused reviews additionally reported 26 contact
tests, 33 combined simulator tests, 24 actuator/FastSAC tests and 133 object
snapshot-related tests passing.  `py_compile`, `git diff --check`, launcher
syntax and the targeted launcher guards passed.  An isolated real-L40S Warp
test then JIT-compiled the modified kernel and passed on a non-default Torch
stream.  It covered first-capture targeted mode, full mode, unsorted/duplicate
IDs, empty IDs and a camera pose change; selected targeted rows were bitwise
equal to the corresponding full-capture rows and inactive rows were unchanged.

