======================================================================

A sequential collider-only A/B was run on the three dedicated safe nodes with
the frozen source
`src-9bf523be1bd5f871106e9847105a78856d283a54fef17074f7db2389f51126b3`
(archive SHA256
`70c62db32a3e1942534b4c8273706022a730ca9057ce1ca94613c57bbd9686e4`).
Both runs used world size 24, 64 env/GPU, seed 42, 32 fresh iterations, one
epoch, MB16, identical teacher/data/runtime/communication settings and object
reporters disabled:

  * decomposition baseline: W&B `zihanw22/carry-any/cbb3wnff`, runtime
    manifest SHA256
    `cd794f5e242bcc84a8a4e6b3dd092a93820c907468d551a2cdcaa4c370dd510f`;
  * hull candidate: W&B `zihanw22/carry-any/cdwlh649`, runtime manifest SHA256
    `e83c110fc77cbe64730b6d4aff10305ed6854ae8aa4c330562c80e947acc4e1a`.

A recursive manifest comparison had exactly one semantic difference:
`object_loader.semantics.collider_type` changed from
`convex_decomposition` to `convex_hull`.  Over W&B steps 3--31 (n=29,
mean/sample standard deviation), the measured performance was:

  metric                    decomposition       convex hull       change
  collection seconds        2.339549/0.268380    2.316159/0.154743  -1.000%
  learning seconds          0.646516/0.011486    0.645305/0.008015  -0.187%
  total seconds             2.986065/0.266310    2.961465/0.154968  -0.824%
  total FPS                 12415.10/820.96      12476.62/579.76    +0.496%

The paired total-time 95% interval crossed zero and hull was faster on only
15/29 steps, so this was not a credible material speedup.  At the same time,
mean reward changed 2.20803 -> 2.03322 (-7.92%), mean episode length 24.2197
-> 22.5863 (-6.74%), bad-tracking fraction rose 5.87%, reference angular
velocity error rose 10.84%, and terminal fixed-BC mu-MSE changed 0.434331 ->
0.726607 (+67.29%).  This single-seed canary cannot prove universal quality
degradation, but the approximately 1% timing change is far below the
predeclared 5% collider threshold and carries clear physical/learning risk.
`convex_hull` is therefore rejected as the production default;
`convex_decomposition` remains canonical.  Hull remains only an explicit
diagnostic opt-in.

Both runs naturally completed 32/32, W&B finished, terminal checkpoint/ONNX
and 24-rank state closure were present, and all three nodes released their
tmux sessions and GPUs.  The protected r22/r23 jobs were not touched.

======================================================================
59. ROLLOUT-REFERENCE GATHER CANARY REJECTED AND EXACTLY REVERTED
======================================================================

The final audited reward hotspot was rollout-reference sampling.  The active
sample is cached once per reward computation but gathers 17 bank fields.  The
old helper first materializes `[E,T,...]` with `index_select` and then gathers
one timestep.  At E=64, T=320 and 14 tracked bodies, those intermediates total
18,124,800 bytes/control step, approximately 435 MB/rank over a 24-step
rollout.  A direct `(clip,timestep)` implementation was bitwise equal on valid
inputs and its CPU helper microbenchmark was 0.679156 -> 0.341717 ms.  It also
preserved malformed negative-index failure by mapping negatives to a positive
out-of-bounds index.

The candidate was frozen as
`src-3cfee7d95d3ecdf3072fbc8c7fa87d94a9d87ab07bfd0cd5760e1ad74ce45bd9`
(archive SHA256
`4c09a689eed9f27ecbf96d7229efb2b0bd8ea171941779bd43320bcc91cae7e4`).
Recursive comparison with the decomposition baseline snapshot found only the
gather helper and its test file different.  The 24-rank canary was session
`simcol_reference_direct_gather_ws24_20260717_c`, W&B
`zihanw22/carry-any/nfnvnumc`.  It passed all 24 worker provenance/startup
gates and naturally completed 32/32.  Its checkpoint SHA256 was
`2fcb0ff5603c3d103549366fc180f90bd907ae53091ed0e17f0c2130ed44d801`,
ONNX SHA256 was
`3c8163458cde13e0eca4abad7d7f31655944627a414d3f62217c0051d14b88f7`,
and terminal fixed-BC mu-MSE was `0.46001269744970658` over 4096 samples.

The same W&B steps 3--31 (n=29) did not show a gain:

  metric                    baseline            candidate          change
  collection seconds        2.339549/0.268380    2.406391/0.313317  +2.857%
  learning seconds          0.646516/0.011486    0.643548/0.009386  -0.459%
  total seconds             2.986065/0.266310    3.049939/0.311563  +2.139%
  total FPS                 12415.10/820.96      12186.59/1023.54   -1.841%

The paired interval crossed zero, but the candidate supplied no positive
end-to-end evidence.  A post-run synchronized L40S microbenchmark explained
the regression: the old 17-field batch took 0.489 ms, unguarded direct indexing
0.151 ms, but preserving fail-closed negative-index semantics separately in
each of the 17 calls took 1.148 ms because it launched two additional
`torch.where` kernels per field.  Hoisting the guards once reduced the helper
batch to 0.213 ms, but its theoretical saving was only about 6.6 ms per full
iteration and did not justify another 24-GPU experiment or a more complex
internal contract.

The direct-gather code and tests were therefore exactly reverted.  Byte
comparison against the accepted decomposition snapshot returned equality for
both files, and the post-revert focused reward/contact/rollover suite passed 45
tests with `git diff --check` clean.  The exact lifecycle stop then verified on
all three nodes: matching tmux=0, GPU compute applications=0 and listeners on
30701/30702=0.  The final worktree retains the proven R6 observation reuse and
the exact contact/rollover fixes, but not R7 reset fusion, convex hull, deferred
finite checks or direct reference gather.

One visibility caveat remains explicit: the scientific batch launcher seals
`convex_decomposition` and object reporters off, and both values are present
in the runtime asset manifest/W&B/checkpoint provenance.  A bare direct
RunSim invocation with neither environment variable set currently follows the
runtime library defaults (`convex_hull`, reporters on).  Such a bare invocation
must not be presented as the training-physics reproduction; direct scientific
evaluation must export the two recorded values until the direct launcher owns
and seals the same contract.

======================================================================
60. KINEMATIC REPLAY IMMUTABLE-BANK PATH FIX
======================================================================

The dedicated port-18093 replay initially showed the grey `Training G1` in
its default joint pose.  This was a viewer path-resolution failure, not a
motion-data failure.  All 30 rollout metadata entries still named the legacy
flat path `_single_slot_motion_bank/<clip>.npz`, while the exact r22/r23 input
bank is immutable and content-addressed under
`_single_slot_motion_bank/by-source/737945...a5d4/<clip>.npz`.  The viewer
ignored its explicitly supplied `--original-motion-dir`, resolved 0/30 grey
robot trajectories, and deliberately entered `showing default G1 pose`.

The grey-robot loader now treats the explicitly supplied original-motion
directory as authoritative and only falls back to legacy rollout metadata
when the requested clip is absent there.  It does not modify immutable data,
create compatibility symlinks, or glob across potentially ambiguous source
generations.

Data-integrity checks independently showed that all 30 clips contain finite
`(319, 36)` floating-base joint trajectories; every clip has time-varying
DOFs, maximum per-joint ranges are 1.8757--2.5348 rad, root travel is
1.795--2.065 m, and object travel is 1.787--2.097 m.  A direct loader
regression resolved and loaded 30/30 exact files as 319 frames and 29 named
joints with no default-pose status.  `py_compile` and `git diff --check`
passed.  The exact tmux session `as_kinematic_replay_18093` was then restarted
on the same port; HTTP returned 200 and the process uses no GPU.  No training
job, port 18092 viewer, immutable bank, or unrelated process was changed.

======================================================================
61. ONE-MOTION TRANSITION LINEAGE AND FORMAL REPLAY GATE
======================================================================

The first filtered `unscale__any_ball_29` attempt exposed a second, distinct
single-motion bug.  Filtering the 30-clip scientific source down to one active
clip had also caused the derived solid/single-slot/rank-shard artifacts to be
classified as a native standalone one-clip source.  The live command therefore
materialized a static default-pose splice directly into the motion tensors.
That is not the timeline used by the original 30-clip source: the source keeps
the motion tensors intact and performs an IsaacSim runtime prepend.  Reusing
the active objective count to choose both loss weighting and transition
implementation made the replay wrong and would have changed the motion clock,
reward horizon, reset window and contact-sidecar alignment during training.

Transition lineage is now an independent immutable record with the exact
schema

  {"version": 1,
   "source_clip_count": 30,
   "source_semantics": "global_multi_clip_runtime"}

for all three filtered objectives.  It is inferred before filtering and is
carried through the solid-bank object map, single-slot generation, rank-shard
manifest and rank payload.  MotionLoader, MotionCommand and the kinematic
replay all consume the same record.  Partial presence, malformed fields,
source-count/semantics inconsistency, or disagreement between the loader and
rank payload now fails closed.  Legacy artifacts with no explicit record keep
their previous fallback only for compatibility.

This lineage does not change the learning objective.  Each formal ws8 run has
exactly one global objective clip duplicated once per rank: global clip count
1, cover count 8, inverse-cover mass 0.125 and distributed loss weight 1.0 on
every rank.  The explicit source count 30 controls only the transition
implementation; it cannot accidentally introduce a 1/30 loss scale.  Focused
bank/shard/runtime/visual regressions on the exact frozen source passed 102
tests in 6.94 seconds.

