======================================================================
62. R25 FLAT-NCCL FREEZE REPRODUCTION AND EXACT ROLLBACK
======================================================================

The first formal one-motion r25 launches all passed 8/8 startup and did real
training, but then hung at different points inside `training_step`: ball at
iteration 4, bin at 7, and barrel at 86.  In every case all eight workers
remained alive in futex waits, all eight GPUs retained approximately 26 GiB,
and GPU utilization fell to 0--1 percent with no further log progress.  There
was no Python traceback, non-finite metric, CUDA OOM, NCCL diagnostic, Xid,
ECC error, disk pressure or host-memory pressure.  The different clips and
failure iterations argue against, but do not strictly exclude, a fixed
clip-specific or fixed-iteration motion/object branch.

A pure eight-process communication canary then used the exact pinned NCCL
2.26.2 library (SHA256
`e4a7aee9c3eecf53fac780441d2f03b578ab8db8874b71f8e391bcec7adb2899`),
approximately 3.08 million float gradient elements, 16 NCCL payload
all-reduces per outer round and interleaved Gloo controls.  It reproduced
stochastic freezes on
`10.99.1.21` and `10.99.1.122`; `10.99.0.141` completed separate 3,200- and
4,800-operation samples, which is consistent with a low-probability transport
failure rather than a permanently bad node.  The same nodes have matching
kernel, driver, topology and healthy 8xL40S hardware.  Because the pure canary
contains no student policy, W&B, simulator collection or motion bank, this
strongly implicates the single-node flat-NCCL/mixed-control path.  It does not
by itself prove that the r25 freezes had an identical root cause.

Only the three exact r25 sessions were stopped.  Their launch tokens, tmux
sessions, torchrun/workers, compute applications and rendezvous/provenance
ports were checked closed before reuse.  No unrelated session or GPU process
was touched, and none of the stale r25 W&B identities was resumed.

