The first summary rebind attempt exposed an important live-W&B detail. A
Public API summary mutation can be overwritten by the primary training
writer's already-consolidated startup summary on a later metric flush. The
transaction initially stopped before deleting the original old file when it
could not prove the new summary path. A second attempt verified the new
history bytes before retirement but then observed the primary writer restore
the stale summary path; this was not accepted as complete, and the dangling
summary was not left as the final state.

The durable correction attached a short-lived client to each *existing*
rank-zero `wandb-core` service via its exact local Unix service socket and run
ID. It updated the consolidated summary owned by the live primary writer to
the already-uploaded history media JSON; it did not call `finish`, log another
history row, or alter training state. The rollout binding persisted on the
first attached update. The original writer had one older queued summary flush,
so its exact service was updated a second time after that queue drained. A
subsequent 25-second/multiple-iteration API window proved both primary
summaries remained bound to their history paths with
`replay/history_backed=true` and the exact history step.

Only after byte and primary-summary verification were both old
`replay_summary_<sha-prefix>.mp4` objects deleted. The final fresh API audit
found exactly one `vis/replay` history row, exactly one MP4 run file, and an
identical summary/history path, SHA256, and size on each run. Both run states
remained `running`. At 2026-07-20 07:17 UTC, all original ranks had advanced
uniformly to iteration 291 and all rollout ranks to iteration 334; all eight
sessions still had eight GPU compute processes per node, no exit file, and
zero post-first-progress hard-error additions. Thus replay promotion neither
stopped nor restarted training and left no duplicate long-term media storage.

