[2026-07-25] audited HIL-style task-sparse straight-line reward direction

The user clarified that straight walking must be learned from training reward,
not supplied by a simulator-state command adapter at deployment. The HIL
heading objective is a per-step low-dimensional task reward,
`w_vel*exp(-alpha*(v_target-d_hat^T v)^2) + w_face*(f_hat^T q_hat)`.
The RSS follow-up similarly calls its position/orientation error plus reach
bonus sparse relative to dense full-body imitation; neither objective is a
purely terminal binary reward.

The exact `34qv1qqp/model_40000.pt` config shows that its "pure RL" label means
PPO without teacher, BC, or distillation. Its active reward remains dense
reference tracking for root/body pose and velocity plus object pose, with
positive maximum weight seven and bad-tracking termination. Its command mode
is `tracking_error`, so training automatically exposes a nonzero reference
relative dyaw after heading drift. A deployment input held at
`[0.15,0,0,0]` does not provide that correction. The run is therefore not an
HIL-style goal/task-sparse experiment.

The scientifically appropriate next contract is to warm start from a
pickup/carry tracking checkpoint and train parallel tracking and task
environments. Tracking environments retain dense imitation for motion quality.
Task environments receive deployment-faithful sample-and-hold commands after a
stable lift, remove full-body reference tracking, and optimize desired-direction
velocity, lateral velocity, initial-heading facing, cross-track error, yaw rate,
object hold, and waypoint/reach bonuses. Only the privileged critic may receive
a task indicator. World pose can be used to compute reward without entering
the actor or deployment interface.

There is a necessary observability boundary. The current feed-forward actor
sees body angular velocity but a fixed dyaw of zero contains no accumulated
heading error. Reward can teach strong yaw-rate damping and reduce
self-induced drift, but strict recovery to the original world heading after an
arbitrary external rotation cannot be guaranteed. HIL supplies target heading
and facing to the actor. Strong recovery would require a dynamic relative
heading error in the same command slots or a recurrent/history policy that can
integrate yaw rate.

To prevent future evaluation confusion, both the recorder
`manual_forward_heading_lock` flag and the core after-lift `heading_lock`
argument now default to false. Raw constant command is deployment-faithful;
the simulator world-heading adapter is explicit opt-in diagnostic behavior.
The focused suite remains 90 passing tests, compileall passed, and
`git diff --check` passed. No training, W&B mutation, or GPU use occurred.

